  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, specifically preferring more right-skewed lotteries, which in this context means a higher concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes. However, in this case, the more right-skewed lottery (B) actually has a higher probability of the best outcome (0), and a lower probability of any loss—making it appear 'safer'. The individual’s reasoning aligns with preferring Lottery B due to its higher chance of no loss and lower chance of any loss occurring. While the choice aligns with the decision reason, the justification is based on loss avoidance and the desire to escape any loss, not on an evaluation of the skewness of the distribution. The individual does not mention or appear to consider the shape of the distribution, concentration of probabilities across favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes in a skewness framework, or compare the lotteries in terms of asymmetry. Instead, the reasoning is probability-outcome focused: higher chance of zero = better. Although the conclusion matches what the decision reason would recommend, the cognitive process described does not reflect the application of skewness as a rule. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used skewness as a decision reason, even though the outcome matches.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
